From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:09:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] milkflint tests: a missing firmware file's error, the same
 only without the sysfs fallback

mt7915_mcu2_init_no_ram_code and _no_patch request the missing file
once for the error to expect, then check the driver's own request fails
with it. With the firmware loader's sysfs fallback (OpenWrt's kernels
have it), each request waits for procd to answer the uevent, which it
does with -ENOMEM for a file that isn't there, or times out after 60 s
with -ETIMEDOUT. On a slow boot the first answer came near the timeout,
so the two requests sometimes failed differently. The VM now boots with
the fallback ignored; the helper's comment says why it must be.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 mt7915/tests/mcu2-test.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mt7915/tests/mcu2-test.c b/mt7915/tests/mcu2-test.c
index 0e90ce14..70671be4 100644
--- a/mt7915/tests/mcu2-test.c
+++ b/mt7915/tests/mcu2-test.c
@@ -434,7 +434,10 @@ static void mt7915_mcu2_chip_ready(struct mt7915_dev *dev)
 }
 
 /* what loading firmware file @name fails with here (the VM has no MT7986
- * firmware); skips the test if the file is there after all
+ * firmware); skips the test if the file is there after all. The driver's
+ * own request fails the same way only if neither asks userspace: with the
+ * loader's sysfs fallback, procd's answer races its 60 s timeout, so the
+ * VM boots with the fallback ignored (kernel.firmware_config).
  */
 static int mt7915_mcu2_fw_missing(struct kunit *test, struct mt7915_dev *dev,
 				  const char *name)
